# Line endings, decided here rather than by whichever machine last touched a
# file.
#
# This tree is edited on macOS and on Windows and deployed to Linux, and it
# arrived with 17 files in CRLF and 124 in LF -- a split nobody chose and that
# nothing kept consistent. The cost is not only tidiness: a Python edit made on
# one machine rewrites every line of a CRLF file, so a one-line change arrives
# as a whole-file diff and `git blame` stops answering.
#
# `text=auto` normalises to LF *in the repository*; `eol=lf` also checks out LF
# everywhere, which is what the deployed files need and what every editor in
# use here handles.
* text=auto eol=lf

# These reach the station and are read by Linux. A CRLF in a shell script is
# not untidy, it is `bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M` on a host reached over
# AnyDesk; in a unit file it is a value with a stray carriage return in it.
# Already true of all of them today -- stated so it stays true.
*.sh       text eol=lf
*.service  text eol=lf
*.timer    text eol=lf
*.target   text eol=lf

# Never converted, never diffed as text. `text=auto` inspects content and would
# leave these alone on its own, but a heuristic is the wrong thing to rest a
# 6 MB PDF on.
*.pdf binary
# Saved page, not a template: it carries NUL bytes and is binary by content
# despite the extension, so the blanket `*.html` handling must not reach it.
docs/references/DIDBase-rules-of-the-road.html binary
